course: dictionary
lesson: 11340
title: Word parts and places
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sometimes a word is only part of a longer word. Zealander is mostly the end of New Zealander, a person who comes from New Zealand. The word before it, New, is the clue that tells you which Zealander is meant. «Anzac Day remembers Australians and New Zealanders who served and died in wars.» Both words name people from a country, so zealander must mean a person from New Zealand.
explain: On its own, zealander can mean a person from Zealand, a region of Denmark or the Netherlands. Always check the words around it. If the text is about New Zealand, the meaning is clear. If the text is about Denmark or the Netherlands, it may mean someone from Zealand instead.
explain: Words also show how people talk differently in each place. «Australians call thongs and New Zealanders call jandals.» Thongs and jandals are both names for flip-flops, so the two countries use different words for the same thing. When you read this, ask: which country is the sentence about?
fact: A New Zealander is a person who comes from New Zealand.
fact: New Zealand is a country in the Pacific Ocean, south-east of Australia.
fact: New Zealanders are often called Kiwis.
fact: The first people of New Zealand are the Māori, and about 1 in 6 New Zealanders is Māori.
fact: On its own, Zealander can mean a person from Zealand, a region of Denmark or the Netherlands (Zeeland).
exercise: What is a New Zealander? | A person who comes from Australia | A kind of flip-flop | A type of sandal | *A person who comes from New Zealand
exercise: Which country is home to New Zealanders? | Australia | Scotland | Denmark | *New Zealand
exercise: The first people of New Zealand are called what? | Jandals | *Māori | Anzacs | Kiwis
test: In a text about Denmark, «Zealander» most likely means what? | A soldier at Gallipoli | A flip-flop | *A person from Zealand | A person from New Zealand
test: Why should you read the words around a word? | Long words are always about countries | You should skip all names | *The words around it show which meaning fits | Capital letters tell you the meaning
exam: What is a zealander on its own? | A kind of sandal | A country in the Pacific Ocean | A bird that lives in New Zealand | *A person from Zealand, a region of Denmark or the Netherlands
